<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Not sandboxed.

	     This is a Developer ID application, distributed directly and updated
	     through Sparkle, which rules out the App Store — so the sandbox was
	     buying defence in depth rather than a distribution requirement, and it
	     was buying it at a steep price here. The app opens multi-gigabyte
	     datasets from wherever the user keeps them, materialises them from
	     iCloud and Dropbox through NSFileCoordinator, walks user-chosen
	     directory trees for batch runs, and loads third-party plugin bundles
	     that it had already had to disable library validation to permit.

	     What actually protects the user is unchanged: the hardened runtime
	     stays on, the app is signed with a Developer ID and notarised, and
	     Gatekeeper checks both. Sparkle in a sandboxed app additionally needs
	     its Downloader and Installer XPC services embedded and separately
	     entitled, which is a class of failure that only appears in a signed,
	     notarised build — the worst place to discover it. -->

	<!-- The hardened runtime otherwise refuses to load any code not signed by
	     this app's team, which is every plugin. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
